      "name": "ApplyPaletteTransformToVideoFrame",
      "signature": "void ApplyPaletteTransformToVideoFrame(int frameWidth, int stride)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined",
      "comment": "Applies palette transformation to video frame data.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if global video buffer pointer is valid\n   (DAT_6f8cf780)\n2. If valid, calculate destination offset based on frame width, global stride,\n   and base buffer offset\n3. Verify frameWidth is non-zero; if zero, skip processing\n4. For each row in frame (frameWidth iterations):\n   a. If stride is non-zero, loop through stride bytes\n   b. Load each source byte and use as index into palette LUT (DAT_6f8cf680)\n   c. Store transformed byte at calculated destination offset\n   d. Advance destination offset by global stride value\n   e. Continue to next row\n5. Return after processing all rows\n\nParameters:\nframeWidth: int - Number of rows to process in video frame\nstride: int - Number of bytes per row to transform\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If DAT_6f8cf780 (video buffer) is null, function returns immediately\n- If frameWidth is zero, function returns without processing\n- If stride is zero, inner loop is skipped but outer loop continues\n- Palette LUT at DAT_6f8cf680 maps 256 source values to 256 palette indices\n- All memory access uses global stride value (DAT_6f8d01c8) for proper alignment\n\nStructure Layout:\nVideo Buffer Access Pattern:\nOffset  Size  Access Purpose\n----    ----  ------ -------\n+0      var   Source pixel data for palette lookup\n+0      var   Destination offset for transformed pixels",
      "name_source": "LoD/PD2",
